---
import { ISSUES_URL, LICENSE_URL, REPO_URL } from '../config'
---

<section id="faq" class="section" aria-labelledby="faq-title">
  <div class="container layout">
    <div class="intro">
      <p class="eyebrow">FAQ</p>
      <h2 id="faq-title">Questions, answered</h2>
      <p class="lead">
        Something else on your mind? <a href={ISSUES_URL}>Ask on the FXM issue tracker</a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="list">
      <details>
        <summary>Is FXM free?</summary>
        <div class="answer">
          <p>
            Yes. FXM is free and open source under the
            <a href={LICENSE_URL}>Apache-2.0 license</a>, with no subscriptions, no in-app purchases
            and no ads from FXM. Anyone can read the
            <a href={REPO_URL}>source code on GitHub</a>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>Why does the web app need an extension?</summary>
        <div class="answer">
          <p>
            For your safety, browsers don't let one website read data from another unless that site
            allows it. Music services such as YouTube Music don't, so a regular website can't search
            or stream from them. The free FXM Connect extension makes those requests from your own
            browser, for the web app. Nothing goes through FXM servers, because there aren't any.
          </p>
          <p>
            Without the extension, the web app still works with free sources (Internet Archive and
            Jamendo) and invites you to add FXM Connect for YouTube Music. The desktop and Android
            apps don't need it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>Which browsers does it work in?</summary>
        <div class="answer">
          <p>
            FXM Connect is for Chrome, Edge and Brave. You install it from the zip on the GitHub
            release with <strong>Load unpacked</strong> (<a href="#get-web">see the steps</a>). A
            Firefox add-on is coming. In other browsers, such as Safari, the web app plays free
            music from Internet Archive and Jamendo.
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>Is there an iPhone app?</summary>
        <div class="answer">
          <p>
            No, and none is planned for now. On iPhone and iPad you can use the web app in Safari
            and add it to your Home Screen. FXM Connect isn't available there, so the web app plays
            from free sources such as Internet Archive and Jamendo.
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>Is my data private?</summary>
        <div class="answer">
          <p>
            Yes. FXM has no accounts, no analytics and no servers, so there's nothing for FXM to
            collect. Your library, history and settings stay in your browser or in the app.
          </p>
          <p>
            When you search or play, your device talks directly to the services you use, and they
            see those requests as if you visited them yourself. The
            <a href="/privacy">privacy page</a>
            lists every service and what it's used for.
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>Can I get lossless audio?</summary>
        <div class="answer">
          <p>
            Yes, where the source has it. Internet Archive and Jamendo offer real lossless FLAC, and
            FXM can save it without any loss. YouTube Music doesn't offer lossless audio, so a FLAC,
            ALAC or WAV made from it is a conversion. FXM labels those as converted from a lossy
            source, so you always know what you have.
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>Is FXM affiliated with YouTube or Spotify?</summary>
        <div class="answer">
          <p>
            No. FXM is an independent open-source project, not affiliated with or endorsed by
            YouTube, Google, Spotify or any music service. See the
            <a href="/disclaimer">disclaimer</a>
            for details.
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
.layout {
  display: grid;
  gap: var(--space-10);
}

.intro {
  display: grid;
  gap: var(--space-4);
  align-content: start;
}

.list {
  border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border);
}

details {
  border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
}

summary {
  position: relative;
  padding: var(--space-5) var(--space-10) var(--space-5) 0;
  border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
  font-size: var(--text-lg);
  font-weight: 650;
  line-height: var(--leading-snug);
  list-style: none;
  cursor: pointer;
}

summary::-webkit-details-marker {
  display: none;
}

summary::after {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  top: 50%;
  right: var(--space-2);
  width: 0.6rem;
  height: 0.6rem;
  border-right: 2px solid var(--color-text-muted);
  border-bottom: 2px solid var(--color-text-muted);
  transform: translateY(-75%) rotate(45deg);
}

@media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
  summary::after {
    transition: transform var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-standard);
  }
}

details[open] summary::after {
  border-color: var(--color-accent);
  transform: translateY(-25%) rotate(-135deg);
}

summary:hover {
  color: var(--color-accent);
}

.answer {
  display: grid;
  gap: var(--space-3);
  padding: 0 var(--space-10) var(--space-6) 0;
  color: var(--color-text-muted);
}

@media (min-width: 60rem) {
  .layout {
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1.8fr);
    gap: var(--space-16);
  }

  .intro {
    position: sticky;
    top: calc(var(--header-height) + var(--space-8));
  }
}
</style>
